<h1>Orcraft Test</h1>
<p>A test is the smallest thing in Orcraft worth building on its own. It needs no group, no suite and no
  endpoint: a single Python file with a test class in it can be run from the command line, and the same
  file, unchanged, is what the controller loads later.</p>

<p>Every test is three classes - a config, a result and the test itself:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTest,
	OrcraftTestCase,
	OrcraftTestConfig,
	OrcraftTestResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftResultField,
)


class MyTestConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Test")
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="Verifies that the system under test responds correctly.")
	iterations = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=3, label="Iterations")
	threshold = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=100, label="Threshold")


class MyTestResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")


class MyTest(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: MyTestConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or MyTestConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
		case.result = MyTestResult()

		measured = measure(iterations=self.config.iterations)
		case.result.measured_value = measured

		self.checkpoint(
			measured <= self.config.threshold,
			f"Measured {measured}, within the threshold of {self.config.threshold}.",
			f"Measured {measured}, above the threshold of {self.config.threshold}.",
		)

		return case.result


def main() -> None:
	MyTest().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<h2 id="config-class">The config class</h2>
<p>The config declares everything about the test that can be configured from outside it. Each field is a
  class attribute built from the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/"><code>OrcraftConfigField</code></a>
  namespace, and inside the test it reads as an ordinary attribute -
  <code>self.config.iterations</code> - holding either the configured value or the declared default.</p>

<p>Subclassing <code>OrcraftTestConfig</code> means the config starts out with fields it did not declare.
  Overriding one, as <code>name</code> and <code>description</code> are overridden above, replaces its
  default without changing what it is for.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Inherited field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Test"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs. Not editable from the GUI. The framework prefixes it with <code>Test: </code> in the log.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the test does. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Test version, recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>test_endpoint</code></td>
      <td><code>"localhost"</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint this test executes against - see <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Orcraft Endpoints</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>matrix</code></td>
      <td><code>None</code></td>
      <td>The dimensions that expand into cases - see <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/">Orcraft Matrix</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td><code>1</code></td>
      <td>How many times the whole test repeats - see <a class="text-accent" href="#cycles">Cycles</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>case_number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td><code>1</code></td>
      <td>How many times each case repeats - see <a class="text-accent" href="#cycles">Cycles</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>upload_test_package</code></td>
      <td><code>"if_missing"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the test package is uploaded to the endpoint before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>update_endpoint_app</code></td>
      <td><code>"on_mismatch"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the endpoint application is updated before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>palette</code></td>
      <td><code>"gray"</code></td>
      <td>Log color palette applied to the test and everything it runs: <code>default</code>, <code>classic</code>, <code>gray</code> or <code>brown</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td><code>HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config and result tables.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>start_time</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>When the run started. Stamped by the framework at run time - leave it alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_tab</code></td>
      <td><code>0</code></td>
      <td>Indent level of this component's log block. Internal: the framework sets it so nested components indent beneath their parent.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>palette</td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Fields sitting at their default - a single cycle, an empty matrix - are hidden from the run panel
  rather than shown as clutter, and the endpoint policy fields only appear for a test that actually uses
  endpoints.</p>

<p><code>upload_test_package</code> takes one of three values:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code> takes one of three values:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="result-class">The result class</h2>
<p>The result declares what the test reports. Its fields come from the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/results-and-statuses/"><code>OrcraftResultField</code></a>
  namespace, and like the config it starts out with fields it did not declare. Unlike the config, most of
  these are written by the framework, not by you:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Inherited field</th>
      <th>Written by</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td>Framework, via checkpoints and descriptions</td>
      <td><code>PASS</code>, <code>FAIL</code>, <code>ERROR</code> or <code>ABORT</code>. Assign it directly only for <code>ERROR</code> - see <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/results-and-statuses/#setting-a-status">Setting a status</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>failure_description</code></td>
      <td>You, or a failing checkpoint</td>
      <td>Why the system under test was judged incorrect. Setting it moves a passing result to <code>FAIL</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>error_description</code></td>
      <td>You, or the framework on an uncaught exception</td>
      <td>Why the test could not complete.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>warning_description</code></td>
      <td>You</td>
      <td>Something worth flagging that does not change the status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code></td>
      <td>Framework</td>
      <td>When the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td>Framework</td>
      <td>How long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code>passed_cases</code>, <code>failed_cases</code>, <code>error_cases</code>, <code>warning_cases</code></td>
      <td>Framework</td>
      <td>Per-case counters, summed from the cases that ran and carried up into the group and suite results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td>You, by overriding the default</td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally or vertically. Test results default to vertical because the counters make a horizontal row too wide.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>Framework</td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under any of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and status comparisons, the counters the group and suite
    roll up, and the controller's result columns silently break. Only <code>log_orientation</code> is
    meant to be overridden, and only to change its default.</p>
</div>

<p>Declaring a result class is optional. A test that has nothing of its own to report can leave
  <code>case.result</code> alone and still produce a complete pass or fail. Declaring one is what makes
  measured values appear in the log tables, in the controller and in exported results.</p>

<h2 id="run-case">The test body</h2>
<p><code>_run_case</code> is the only method a test must implement. The framework calls it once per case
  per cycle, hands it the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#cases">case</a>, and
  expects the case result back:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
	case.result = MyTestResult()
	...
	return case.result') }}

<p>Assigning <code>case.result</code> to your own result subclass is what puts the extra result fields in
  play - without it the case reports only the inherited ones. Everything else about the body is ordinary
  Python: call into the system under test however you like, record what you measured on
  <code>case.result</code>, and assert with
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/results-and-statuses/#checkpoints"><code>checkpoint</code></a>.</p>

<p>A case body does not have to be small. When it grows past what reads well in one method, split it into
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/steps/">steps</a> and
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flows</a> - but that is a decision to make when the
  test needs it, not up front.</p>

<h2 id="cycles">Cycles</h2>
<p>Repetition is configuration, not code. Two independent counters on the test config control it, and
  both are editable from the controller before a run:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td>How many times the whole test - all of its cases - repeats.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>case_number_of_cycles</code></td>
      <td>How many times each individual case repeats before moving to the next one.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The difference is the order of execution. Test cycles repeat the sequence: every case once, then every
  case again. Case cycles repeat in place: the first case three times, then the second case three times.
  For a flaky interaction, case cycles hammer the same conditions; for wear over time, test cycles walk
  the whole matrix repeatedly.</p>

<p>A test with four cases, two test cycles and three case cycles executes the case body twenty-four
  times. Both counters are hidden from the run panel while they sit at one, so a test that does not
  repeat does not advertise the fields.</p>

<p>Cycles are a test-level idea. Suites, groups, flows and steps have no cycle counter of their own: a
  suite repeats because the tests inside it do, and a flow repeats because the case that calls it does.
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">Lifecycle hooks</a> gives each cycle scope its
  own setup and teardown when per-cycle state has to be rebuilt.</p>

<h2 id="lifecycle">Setup and teardown</h2>
<p>Work that must happen around the cases rather than inside them - launching an application, opening a
  connection, generating shared inputs - belongs in a lifecycle hook rather than at the top of
  <code>_run_case</code>:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def _setup(self) -> None:
	self.app = MyApplication(self.config.app_folder)
	self.app.start()

def _teardown(self) -> None:
	self.app.stop()') }}

<p>Every hook is optional. <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">Orcraft Lifecycle Hooks</a>
  covers all six of them and what happens when one fails.</p>

<h2 id="standalone">Running standalone</h2>
<p>Giving the file a <code>main()</code> makes it directly executable, which is how a test is developed
  before it is added to a group:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def main() -> None:
	MyTest().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<p><code>run()</code> executes the full lifecycle - setup, every case of every cycle, teardown - and logs
  the config table, the per-case results and the test summary exactly as the controller would. There is
  no separate packaging step and no alternate entry point: the file that runs from a terminal is the file
  the controller loads.</p>

<p>Once the test does what you want, add it to a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/groups/">group</a>
  to run it alongside its neighbors.</p>
